Live examples of the `<<dyntable>>` macro on the three `Demo Task` tiddlers. Each table gets its own `stateTiddler`, so their edit mode, sorting and pagination stay independent.

!! Basic table

Read-only view: click a column header to sort, click it again to reverse the order. `tags` is shown as is, other built-in columns get a localised header.

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Task]]" fields:"title status priority due-date tags" stateTiddler:"Playground/basic" editButton:"no" 
class="table-borderless table-lines thead-dark table-tight table-rounded-3 table-sides table-center text-center">>

!! Editable, with actions and pagination

Click the pencil in the caption to toggle edit mode: inline editing, checkbox (marks a task ''Done''), clone and delete columns. The arrow column expands a record to show its text. The column currently used for sorting stays locked in edit mode.

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Task]]" fields:"tbl-checkbox tbl-expand title status priority due-date tbl-clone tbl-delete" pagination:"yes" caption:"Demo tasks" stateTiddler:"Playground/editable">>

!! Numerical summary footer

`footer` adds one computed row per entry, written `calc[:decimals][@columns]`: `sum`, `average`, `count`, `median`, `product`, `min` or `max`. Without `@columns`, every numeric column is calculated. The label is translated and shown in the first column. `footerRows` still gives hand-filled cells if you need them.

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Task]]" fields:"title priority" sortType:"number" footer:"sum average:1 min max" stateTiddler:"Playground/footer">>

!! A table with nothing to do with tasks

The table isn't just for tasks: here are the `role` and `maturity` columns (TW-Base-Fields vocabulary) on ordinary knowledge tiddlers, with no `status` field at all.

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Content]]" fields:"title role maturity" stateTiddler:"Playground/content">>

!! Average or median?

The two answer different questions. The ''average'' spreads the total evenly, so use it when every value counts and they are similar. The ''median'' is the middle value once sorted, so use it when a few extreme values would distort the picture.

''Average is better'' — monthly expenses are steady and the total matters, so the average is the monthly budget to plan for:

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Expense]]" fields:"title amount" sortType:"number" footer:"sum average:1 median:1" stateTiddler:"Playground/expenses">>

''Median is better'' — ticket resolution times in hours: one ticket stuck for 120 hours drags the average to 22.8 h, while the median (3.5 h) describes the usual ticket:

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Ticket]]" fields:"title hours" sortType:"number" footer:"average:1 median:1" stateTiddler:"Playground/tickets">>

!!! Getting a reliable picture

No single figure tells the whole story, so show several: the average and the median together, plus the minimum and the maximum. A large gap between average and median reveals skewed values, and min/max show the extremes the median hides. With few values, or two distinct groups (for example 1, 1, 1, 100, 100), the median alone can mislead.

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Ticket]]" fields:"title hours" sortType:"number" footer:"average:1 median:1 min max" stateTiddler:"Playground/reliable">>

The footer of that table is simply `footer:"average:1 median:1 min max"`.

!! Maths grades

Another non-task use: a list of student records with their grade, to show footer calculations on any numeric field (`grade-maths`), not just `priority`. `@grade-maths` restricts the calculation to that column.

<<dyntable filter:"[tag[Demo Student]]" fields:"title grade-maths" sortType:"number" footer:"average:1@grade-maths median max" stateTiddler:"Playground/grades">>
